# The ikigai-log vocabulary — and, literally, the transreptor's term table.
#
# Two things are declared here that would otherwise be Rust:
#
#   * `log:keyName` binds a line's bare `key=` column to a property, and that
#     property's `rdfs:range` decides whether the value becomes an IRI, an
#     integer, a boolean or a plain literal. The mapping is DATA; the
#     transreption code is mechanical.
#   * `log:minLevel` declares, per class, the lowest level at which entries of
#     that class are written, and `log:rank` orders the ladder. The operator
#     sets one dial; this graph decides what it includes.
#
# So a module introduces new entry types with `rdfs:subClassOf` plus its own
# `log:minLevel` and `log:keyName` bindings, and neither the writer nor the
# transreptor changes. That is the whole extension story.
#
# Namespace note: self-contained under `https://ikigai-rs.dev/ns/log#`, the
# `sig:` precedent from ikigai-sign. NOT part of the published ikigai-rs.dev/ns
# vocabulary and needing no /ns deploy — a term is promoted into
# `vocabulary.ttl` when it earns a second consumer, and log-specific classes
# are expected to stay here.

@prefix log:  <https://ikigai-rs.dev/ns/log#> .
@prefix rdf:  <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#> .
@prefix rdfs: <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix prov: <http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#> .
@prefix xsd:  <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#> .
# The signature terms a `#seal` line transrepts to are ikigai-sign's, not
# ours: sig:contentHash / sig:value / sig:algorithm / sig:signer. Reusing them
# rather than minting parallel terms is what makes ikigai-log the second
# consumer that crate names as its own promotion trigger.
@prefix sig:  <https://ikigai-rs.dev/ns/sign#> .

# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# The spine
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

log:Entry a rdfs:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf prov:Activity ;
    rdfs:label "Entry" ;
    rdfs:comment "One log line. A prov:Activity because every entry records an occurrent — something a process DID, at a time, under an authority. Skolemized as urn:log:{segment}:{seq}; never a blank node." .

log:Segment a rdfs:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf prov:Bundle ;
    rdfs:label "Segment" ;
    rdfs:comment "One log file between rotations, and the named graph its entries land in. prov:Bundle is PROV's own term for a named set of provenance descriptions, so segment-as-named-graph needs no invention here." .

log:Instance a rdfs:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf prov:SoftwareAgent ;
    rdfs:label "Instance" ;
    rdfs:comment "One logging PROCESS — the attribution key, since the log is per-process rather than per-machine. Two `serve` processes on one host are two instances, and \"what happened on this machine\" is a union." .

# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# Structure — PROV where PROV says exactly what we mean
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#
# Used directly, so not restated as ik:/log: terms:
#
#   prov:startedAtTime     the line's leading timestamp, on EVERY entry (an
#                          instantaneous entry is an activity with no end).
#                          NOT prov:atTime, whose domain is
#                          prov:InstantaneousEvent — putting it on an Activity
#                          infers a class nobody meant.
#   prov:endedAtTime       only where an end is genuinely known.
#   prov:wasAssociatedWith entry → log:Instance, materialized from the segment
#                          header, so per-process attribution costs no bytes
#                          per line.
#   prov:wasDerivedFrom    an analysis finding → the entries it was CONSTRUCTed
#                          from. The loop guard excludes on this, so a derived
#                          event cannot re-trigger the rule that derived it.

log:resolved a rdf:Property ;
    rdfs:subPropertyOf prov:used ;
    rdfs:domain log:Resolution ;
    rdfs:range rdfs:Resource ;
    rdfs:label "resolved" ;
    rdfs:comment "The target IRI this resolution resolved. A sub-property of prov:used, which states the same fact to a reader that knows PROV and not this vocabulary; the specific property exists because a resolution also uses its arguments and only one of the things it used is the thing that was asked for." .

log:invoked a rdf:Property ;
    rdfs:subPropertyOf prov:wasInformedBy ;
    rdfs:domain log:Resolution ;
    rdfs:range log:Resolution ;
    rdfs:label "invoked" ;
    rdfs:comment "This resolution issued that sub-resolution and consumed its representation — the call-tree edge, parent to child. prov:wasInformedBy holds in exactly this direction: the parent used an entity the child generated. Materialized from log:parentSpan where both lines sit in one segment and one process run; across a rotation or a restart the literal spans survive and the edge is recovered by union." .

log:subject a rdf:Property ;
    rdfs:domain log:Entry ;
    rdfs:range rdfs:Resource ;
    rdfs:label "subject" ;
    rdfs:comment "What the entry is ABOUT, as an IRI — the third column of every line whatever its class. Emitted alongside the class-declared log:subjectPredicate refinement, so an ad-hoc query can ask one question of every entry without joining through this graph while a PROV reader still gets the precise reading. The redundancy is deliberate: two triples, both true, no reasoner required." .

log:subjectPredicate a rdf:Property ;
    rdfs:domain rdfs:Class ;
    rdfs:range rdf:Property ;
    rdfs:label "subjectPredicate" ;
    rdfs:comment "Declared ON an entry class: the precise predicate its subject column means (log:resolved for a resolution, prov:wasAssociatedWith for a liveness event). Read from this graph, so a new class refines its subject reading without a code change." .

# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# The term table
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

log:keyName a rdf:Property ;
    rdfs:domain rdf:Property ;
    rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:label "keyName" ;
    rdfs:comment "The bare key as it appears in a line's key=value column. This binding plus the property's rdfs:range IS the term table. A key no property claims is still recorded losslessly — as log:{key} with a plain literal — and flagged log:undeclaredKey, so drift is measurable rather than silent: the same posture as log:Message, and for the same reason." .

log:undeclaredKey a rdf:Property ;
    rdfs:domain log:Entry ;
    rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:label "undeclaredKey" ;
    rdfs:comment "A key this entry carried that no property claims via log:keyName. Hygiene signal, counted by the same standing CONSTRUCT that counts log:Message." .

# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# Levels — the threshold is a vocabulary fact, and so is the comparison
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

log:Level a rdfs:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Level" ;
    rdfs:comment "A verbosity level. Levels are IRIs carrying a log:rank rather than bare strings, so the emit test is one comparison over this graph and a reader can ask what a level MEANS." .

log:rank a rdf:Property ;
    rdfs:domain log:Level ;
    rdfs:range xsd:integer ;
    rdfs:label "rank" ;
    rdfs:comment "Ordering of the ladder: lower includes fewer classes. An entry class is written iff rank(its log:minLevel) <= rank(the segment's level). log:always ranks below every settable level, which is exactly why the always-land set needs no special case in code." .

log:minLevel a rdf:Property ;
    rdfs:domain rdfs:Class ;
    rdfs:range log:Level ;
    rdfs:label "minLevel" ;
    rdfs:comment "Declared ON an entry class: the lowest level at which entries of that class are written. Inherited along rdfs:subClassOf when a class does not declare its own, so an extension gets a sane minimum for free and states one only where it differs." .

log:always a log:Level ;
    log:rank -1 ;
    rdfs:label "always" ;
    rdfs:comment "Below every settable level: these land whatever the dial says. A hash chain is tamper-evident, NOT omission-evident — it proves nothing was altered and cannot prove nothing was left out — so every legitimate source of absence must leave a marker in the chain: level and config changes, seals, chain breaks, rotations, retention tombstones, dropped entries, process start/stop, capability denials, key changes. The same argument rejects sampling as a volume strategy: sampling is holes everywhere with no brackets." .

log:error a log:Level ; log:rank 0 ; rdfs:label "error" .
log:warn  a log:Level ; log:rank 1 ; rdfs:label "warn" .
log:info  a log:Level ; log:rank 2 ; rdfs:label "info" ;
    rdfs:comment "The day-to-day default: notable events, no resolutions." .
log:debug a log:Level ; log:rank 3 ; rdfs:label "debug" ;
    rdfs:comment "Adds resolutions, without cache hits." .
log:trace a log:Level ; log:rank 4 ; rdfs:label "trace" ;
    rdfs:comment "Adds cache hits — the highest-volume, lowest-information events, so they sit at the top of the dial." .

log:level a rdf:Property ;
    rdfs:domain log:Segment ;
    rdfs:range log:Level ;
    rdfs:label "level" ;
    rdfs:comment "The level a segment ran at, from its header. Exactly ONE per segment: a level change takes effect only at a segment boundary, so a verifier reasons per sealed segment — \"this ran at info, so absent resolutions are explained\" — instead of tracking transitions inside sealed content, and a level change can never open a gap inside it." .

# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# Kernel resolutions — the ikigai_core::Tracer mapping
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

log:Resolution a rdfs:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf log:Entry ;
    log:minLevel log:debug ;
    log:subjectPredicate log:resolved ;
    rdfs:label "Resolution" ;
    rdfs:comment "One resolved invocation, as ikigai_core::Tracer reports it. The kernel writes these, which is what makes them trap-free: they are structured by construction rather than by discipline." .

log:CacheHit a rdfs:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf log:Resolution ;
    log:minLevel log:trace ;
    rdfs:label "CacheHit" ;
    rdfs:comment "A resolution the representation cache served. Its own class rather than a boolean on log:Resolution, because a class is what the level dial can exclude — the threshold is per class." .

log:span a rdf:Property ;
    log:keyName "span" ;
    rdfs:domain log:Resolution ;
    rdfs:range xsd:integer ;
    rdfs:label "span" ;
    rdfs:comment "TraceEvent.span — unique within one process run of the kernel, which is why log:ProcessStart is what bounds a span join." .

log:parentSpan a rdf:Property ;
    log:keyName "parent" ;
    rdfs:domain log:Resolution ;
    rdfs:range xsd:integer ;
    rdfs:label "parentSpan" ;
    rdfs:comment "TraceEvent.parent, kept as the literal it was written as. log:invoked is materialized from it where it resolves; the literal remains, so the edge is still recoverable after a union across segments." .

log:worker a rdf:Property ;
    log:keyName "worker" ;
    rdfs:domain log:Resolution ;
    rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:label "worker" ;
    rdfs:comment "The scheduler worker the invocation ran on (TraceEvent.thread). Named `worker`, NOT `thread`: in ikigai a thread is a GOLDEN thread, and a vocabulary that spent that word on an OS thread would mislead every query later written against it." .

log:capability a rdf:Property ;
    log:keyName "cap" ;
    rdfs:domain log:Entry ;
    rdfs:range rdfs:Resource ;
    rdfs:label "capability" ;
    rdfs:comment "One capability IRI of the authority this entry ran under; repeated per scope, absent for full authority. PROV has no term for the authority an activity ran under — this is ikigai's own axis, and it is a large part of why the log is worth more than a trace." .

log:durationMs a rdf:Property ;
    log:keyName "dur" ;
    rdfs:domain log:Entry ;
    rdfs:range xsd:integer ;
    rdfs:label "durationMs" ;
    rdfs:comment "Elapsed milliseconds, from TraceEvent.started/ended. A duration rather than a second absolute timestamp because a threshold is what anyone greps or filters on; prov:endedAtTime is derivable from it and the start, and is materialized only where a consumer needs it." .

# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# The unstructured escape hatch — permitted, honest, and MEASURABLE
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

log:Message a rdfs:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf log:Entry ;
    log:minLevel log:info ;
    rdfs:label "Message" ;
    rdfs:comment "An informational write whose payload is prose. Deliberately allowed — banning it only drives prose into one field of a typed class, where it is invisible — but it is its OWN class, so the unstructured ratio per module is one standing CONSTRUCT away and 90% prose is a legible smell instead of a silent one. Severity is carried by SUBCLASS, never by a level= column, so the class alone decides emission and grep alone finds failures." .

log:Warning a rdfs:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf log:Message ;
    log:minLevel log:warn ;
    rdfs:label "Warning" .

log:Error a rdfs:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf log:Message ;
    log:minLevel log:error ;
    rdfs:label "Error" ;
    rdfs:comment "Prose describing a failure. NOT where a typed error goes: wire v7 errors are already matchable nodes, and re-serializing one as prose is the printf trap wearing a class name." .

log:text a rdf:Property ;
    log:keyName "msg" ;
    rdfs:domain log:Message ;
    rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:label "text" ;
    rdfs:comment "The prose. Quoted and escaped in the line format; a newline inside it is escaped rather than emitted, because one entry is one line is a load-bearing invariant — of the grammar, of grep, and of the hash chain." .

# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# The always-land set — every legitimate source of ABSENCE leaves a marker
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

log:ProcessStart a rdfs:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf log:Entry ;
    log:minLevel log:always ;
    log:subjectPredicate prov:wasAssociatedWith ;
    rdfs:label "ProcessStart" ;
    rdfs:comment "Liveness, and the run boundary that makes span numbers unambiguous: the kernel's span counter restarts with the process, so a span join is scoped between these markers. The absence query needs a positive liveness entry to key off — an append-only log cannot state absence directly." .

log:ProcessStop a rdfs:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf log:Entry ;
    log:minLevel log:always ;
    log:subjectPredicate prov:wasAssociatedWith ;
    rdfs:label "ProcessStop" .

log:ConfigChange a rdfs:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf log:Entry ;
    log:minLevel log:always ;
    rdfs:label "ConfigChange" ;
    rdfs:comment "Every urn:log:config write, not only the level: lowering the level makes a hole, but repointing the destination silences the log entirely, so the always-land rule is about config as a whole." .

log:LevelChange a rdfs:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf log:ConfigChange ;
    log:minLevel log:always ;
    rdfs:label "LevelChange" ;
    rdfs:comment "Brackets the hole a level change creates, so a verifier can say \"between these seals the level was info, therefore absent resolutions are explained\". Without the bracket, an attacker who can lower the level creates a gap the chain then blesses as perfectly intact." .

log:LevelChangeRejected a rdfs:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf log:Entry ;
    log:minLevel log:always ;
    rdfs:label "LevelChangeRejected" ;
    rdfs:comment "A refused level set, landed immediately and at any level — under `locked` mode this is the evidence that an external modification did not take." .

log:Rotation a rdfs:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf log:Entry ;
    log:minLevel log:always ;
    rdfs:label "Rotation" ;
    rdfs:comment "The last entry of a segment that was rolled over rather than stopped. Rotation is VERIFY + SEAL + VALIDATE as one operation: the successor's @prev names the seal this entry precedes, so the marker and the link are written together or not at all. It also ENDS a segment as finally as log:ProcessStop does — a rotated segment is immutable, which is what makes it cacheable, and a reader that knew only about ProcessStop would treat every rotated segment as a live tail forever." .

log:successor a rdf:Property ;
    log:keyName "next" ;
    rdfs:domain log:Rotation ;
    rdfs:range log:Segment ;
    rdfs:label "successor" ;
    rdfs:comment "The segment this rotation opened — the only FORWARD link in the chain, and it is not merely navigation. @prev catches a segment removed from the MIDDLE, because the one after it still names what should have been there; nothing points forward at the LAST segment, so truncating a chain at its head would be free, and the head is the recent past — the part worth erasing. This is what notices. Written because the successor's IRI is derivable at the moment of rotation: it is the instance and the instant, so the file is reserved before the predecessor is sealed and the marker names what actually exists." .

log:Seal a rdfs:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf log:Entry ;
    log:minLevel log:always ;
    rdfs:label "Seal" ;
    rdfs:comment "A signed checkpoint over a run of entries, written as a `#seal first-last <hash> [<alg>:<base64>]` line so it reads as a comment and greps as ^#seal. Its hash and signature reuse sig:contentHash / sig:value / sig:algorithm from ikigai-sign rather than minting parallel terms — which makes ikigai-log the second consumer that crate names as its own promotion trigger, and which is why the digest is TAGGED (sha256:<hex>) at both ends: sharing a predicate while writing two lexical forms of one value means the two never join in a query. The signature is over the ASCII bytes of that tagged hash, so checking it is `urn:sign:verify in={the hash} sig={the graph} key={the public key}` and this crate holds no key. An UNSIGNED seal still localizes tampering, which is why a segment written without a key seals anyway." .

log:firstSequence a rdf:Property ;
    rdfs:domain log:Seal ;
    rdfs:range xsd:integer ;
    rdfs:label "firstSequence" ;
    rdfs:comment "The first entry sequence a seal covers. A seal names a RANGE, and the range is the whole localization claim: tampering is narrowed to \"between seal K and seal K+1\", which is meaningless without both ends. No log:keyName — a seal is its own line shape, not a key=value column." .

log:lastSequence a rdf:Property ;
    rdfs:domain log:Seal ;
    rdfs:range xsd:integer ;
    rdfs:label "lastSequence" ;
    rdfs:comment "The last entry sequence a seal covers, and the entry whose chain hash sig:contentHash carries." .

log:ChainBroken a rdfs:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf log:Entry ;
    log:minLevel log:always ;
    rdfs:label "ChainBroken" ;
    rdfs:comment "A rotation that could not verify its predecessor. An entry, not an exception: the failure belongs in the record it is a failure of — an exception would be caught by whatever was rotating and the fact would leave no trace. Its subject column is the PREDECESSOR that failed to verify; it is written into the segment the rotation opened, because the one it is about has already been sealed." .

log:expectedSeal a rdf:Property ;
    log:keyName "expected" ;
    rdfs:domain log:ChainBroken ;
    rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:label "expectedSeal" ;
    rdfs:comment "The chain head the predecessor actually ends at, tagged (sha256:…). Written beside log:statedSeal so the break is legible from the grepped line alone." .

log:statedSeal a rdf:Property ;
    log:keyName "stated" ;
    rdfs:domain log:ChainBroken ;
    rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:label "statedSeal" ;
    rdfs:comment "What the broken segment's @prev claimed. Together with log:expectedSeal this is the whole of the rotation-seam failure: a forged replacement segment differs here and nowhere else that a hash-only check would look." .

log:finding a rdf:Property ;
    log:keyName "finding" ;
    rdfs:domain log:Entry ;
    rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:label "finding" ;
    rdfs:comment "One verification finding, in the same words `urn:log:verify` prints. Repeated per finding — a key may repeat in a line, and a chain break usually has more than one thing to say." .

log:Tombstone a rdfs:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf log:Entry ;
    log:minLevel log:always ;
    rdfs:label "Tombstone" ;
    rdfs:comment "A segment existed here, sealed thus, removed under this policy at this time. Retention and tamper-evidence otherwise conflict — a deleted segment and a tampered-away one are indistinguishable — and this is the resolution: the chain head outlives the payload, so an as-of query reaching into it gets \"redacted by retention\" rather than silence." .

log:Dropped a rdfs:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf log:Entry ;
    log:minLevel log:always ;
    rdfs:label "Dropped" ;
    rdfs:comment "N entries lost to back-pressure. Never drop silently." .

log:CapabilityDenied a rdfs:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf log:Entry ;
    log:minLevel log:always ;
    rdfs:label "CapabilityDenied" ;
    rdfs:comment "A refused authority is a security fact, so it lands at every level." .

log:KeyChange a rdfs:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf log:Entry ;
    log:minLevel log:always ;
    rdfs:label "KeyChange" ;
    rdfs:comment "Signing-key rotation. Unrecorded, it is a window in which old signatures stop verifying — which is indistinguishable from tampering." .

# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# The configuration — a resource, so it is describable like everything else
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

log:Config a rdfs:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Config" ;
    rdfs:comment "The EFFECTIVE log configuration of one process: built-in defaults ⊕ log.toml ⊕ {app}.log.toml, merged key-wise. Never the contents of one file, which is why it carries log:configLayer rather than a path — the answer to \"why is the level debug?\" should not need a second lookup." .

log:Destination a rdfs:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Destination" ;
    rdfs:comment "Where a segment's lines go. Individuals rather than strings, so a reader can ask what a destination MEANS and a query can join on it." .

log:off a log:Destination ;
    rdfs:label "off" ;
    rdfs:comment "Nothing is written and no segment is opened — the DEFAULT. A one-shot `ikigai -c` that opened a segment would put a process that did nothing into the journal beside the servers, so a host that means to log says so." .

log:console a log:Destination ;
    rdfs:label "console" ;
    rdfs:comment "The process's own diagnostic stream: stderr natively, console.log in a browser. NOT stdout — stdout is the pipeline's data channel, and a log line in it corrupts the composition." .

log:file a log:Destination ;
    rdfs:label "file" ;
    rdfs:comment "A segment file under log:directory." .

log:none a log:Segment ;
    rdfs:label "none" ;
    rdfs:comment "The stand-in for \"no segment is open\" in a log:currentSegment statement. Stated positively rather than by omitting the triple, because an absent statement is indistinguishable from a config face that forgot to emit one." .

log:destination a rdf:Property ;
    rdfs:domain log:Config ;
    rdfs:range log:Destination ;
    rdfs:label "destination" .

log:directory a rdf:Property ;
    rdfs:domain log:Config ;
    rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:label "directory" ;
    rdfs:comment "Where segment files land. Absent means the ikigai data home's `log` directory — resolved by the process that opens a segment, since only it knows its own HOME." .

log:instance a rdf:Property ;
    rdfs:domain log:Config ;
    rdfs:range log:Instance ;
    rdfs:label "instance" .

log:configLayer a rdf:Property ;
    log:keyName "file" ;
    rdfs:domain log:Config ;
    rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:label "configLayer" ;
    rdfs:comment "One config file that contributed to the effective config, lowest precedence first. Provenance, not configuration — which is why the TOML face omits it and the graph face does not. Also bound as the line column `file=`, naming the layer a log:ConfigChange actually wrote." .

log:currentSegment a rdf:Property ;
    rdfs:domain log:Config ;
    rdfs:range log:Segment ;
    rdfs:label "currentSegment" ;
    rdfs:comment "The segment this process is writing right now, or log:none. The config alone cannot answer it: `destination = file` in a process whose host never opened a writer means nothing is being logged." .

log:configuredInstance a rdf:Property ;
    log:keyName "configured" ;
    rdfs:domain log:Instance ;
    rdfs:range log:Instance ;
    rdfs:label "configuredInstance" ;
    rdfs:comment "The instance name that was ASKED for, when a live process already held it and this one took a disambiguated name instead. Two facts, both true — a silent disambiguation would make the header disagree with the config and nothing would say why. Also the line column `configured=` on log:ProcessStart." .

log:processId a rdf:Property ;
    log:keyName "pid" ;
    rdfs:domain log:Entry ;
    rdfs:range xsd:integer ;
    rdfs:label "processId" ;
    rdfs:comment "The OS process id, on log:ProcessStart. It is also what disambiguates a contended instance name: unique among LIVE processes, which is exactly the span a name arbitrates over, and unlike a random token it points at something an operator can go and look at." .

log:changedKey a rdf:Property ;
    log:keyName "key" ;
    rdfs:domain log:ConfigChange ;
    rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:label "changedKey" ;
    rdfs:comment "Which config key a change touched. One entry per key that actually changed: a write that restates a value is not a change, and inventing an entry for it would dilute the ones that are." .

log:fromValue a rdf:Property ;
    log:keyName "from" ;
    rdfs:domain log:ConfigChange ;
    rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:label "fromValue" .

log:toValue a rdf:Property ;
    log:keyName "to" ;
    rdfs:domain log:ConfigChange ;
    rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:label "toValue" .

log:effective a rdf:Property ;
    log:keyName "effective" ;
    rdfs:domain log:ConfigChange ;
    rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:label "effective" ;
    rdfs:comment "When the change takes hold — `next-segment`, always, because a segment has exactly one level for its whole life. Written on the entry rather than left implicit so that a reader of the line alone, grepped out of its file, still knows the change did not apply to the entries around it." .

log:count a rdf:Property ;
    log:keyName "count" ;
    rdfs:domain log:Entry ;
    rdfs:range xsd:integer ;
    rdfs:label "count" .

log:reason a rdf:Property ;
    log:keyName "reason" ;
    rdfs:domain log:Entry ;
    rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:label "reason" .

log:sequence a rdf:Property ;
    log:keyName "seq" ;
    rdfs:domain log:Entry ;
    rdfs:range xsd:integer ;
    rdfs:label "sequence" .

log:segment a rdf:Property ;
    rdfs:domain log:Entry ;
    rdfs:range log:Segment ;
    rdfs:label "segment" .

log:prevSeal a rdf:Property ;
    rdfs:domain log:Segment ;
    rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:label "prevSeal" ;
    rdfs:comment "The prior segment's final seal, from the @prev header — or the token `genesis`, which is always written rather than left absent so that a missing @prev is unambiguously an error. Precisely: the hash on the predecessor's LAST #seal line, or its header hash h0 when it never sealed (a crashed process must not leave its successor unchainable, or a crash and an erasure would look alike). The chain spans rotations; without it, rotation is the seam at which a whole file can be replaced and nothing detects it, and every other test still passes." .
